  "answer_text": "The iconic scene where Del Boy falls through the bar is from the episode **\"Yuppy Love\"**! Here are the key details:\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udcfa The Episode: *\"Yuppy Love\"*\n\"Yuppy Love\" was the first episode of Series 6 of *Only Fools and Horses*, airing on **8th January 1989** on BBC1, with a viewing figure of **13.9 million**.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83c\udfac The Scene\nDel Boy (David Jason) accidentally falls through the bar, attempting to lean up against it while telling Trigger (Roger Lloyd Pack) about what they should do to appear attractive to women.\n\nA barman comes along and lifts up the section that Del was leaning against to get through, but doesn't put it back down again, leaving a gap \u2014 this happens behind Del's back and he doesn't notice at all. Del says \"Play it nice and cool, son. Nice and cool, you know what I mean...\" \u2014 and then leans back and falls straight through the gap onto the floor.\n\nThe moment is made even more perfect by Trigger's shocked reaction, looking around for Del, who is firmly on the floor.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83c\udfad Behind the Scenes\n- The scene was inspired by writer **John Sullivan** watching a man do the exact same thing in a real wine bar, except the man grabbed onto the fixed part of the bar so he didn't fall right over. Sullivan thought it funny for the man's body language, trying to recover his cool.\n\n- Sullivan originally wanted a slip, stumble, and tree-like fall, but **David Jason** thought Del should go all the way over \u2014 starting to go sideways and then going over without looking in the direction of the fall, which Jason thought was the key to the scene. There was a hidden crash mat, but it was a hard shot because it was difficult not to look where he was falling.\n\n- A rehearsal script that went up for auction showed that **Trigger wasn't supposed to be in the scene originally**. However, Roger Lloyd Pack was there on the day and writer John Sullivan decided he should be included after all.\n\n- Roger Lloyd Pack later revealed that Jason never did the fall in rehearsal, so it was a genuine surprise when it happened during filming.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83c\udfc6 Legacy\nDel Boy's fall through the bar has become **one of the most celebrated sitcom scenes ever**. David Jason gets people asking him about that fall all the time, and he's happy to be remembered for something so iconic.",
